# Server
#
# --- Open source / your deployment ---
# Example URLs in comments sometimes use **chat-echo.com** as the maintainers' **reference deployment**.
# For your own instance, set `VITE_*`, `CORS_ORIGIN`, `ECHO_APP_PUBLIC_URL`, and related variables to **your** hosts.
#
# --- Vite / `frontend` (important for `npm run prod` and `vite preview`) ---
# Do not set `NODE_ENV=production` or `NODE_ENV=PRODUCTION` inside any file Vite loads from the
# frontend package (`frontend/.env`, `frontend/.env.*`, `frontend/.env.local`). Vite will warn
# and ignores it for mode selection. Production for the API is set by root `prod:serve` via
# `cross-env NODE_ENV=production` on `npm start`. If you symlink `frontend/.env` → this root
# file, keep `NODE_ENV` out of the symlinked file and use `backend/.env` for backend-only flags,
# or rely on the prod script as above.
#
PORT=3000
HOST=0.0.0.0
LOG_LEVEL=info
# TLS terminates at Caddy and traffic to Node is HTTP: set true so `ENFORCE_HTTPS` (on by
# default in production) trusts `X-Forwarded-Proto` / forwarded scheme. Required for e.g.
# chat-echo.com → proxy → Echo. Never set on a host where clients can reach the API directly
# without a proxy that strips forged `X-Forwarded-*` headers.
# ECHO_TRUST_PROXY=true
# When your edge proxy emits CSP + HSTS on every response (including `/api/`) and you must avoid
# duplicate headers, set true. Unusual for Caddy split-`handle` configs — see
# docs/operations/http-observatory-headers.md.
# ECHO_EDGE_SECURITY_HEADERS=true
# LAN + voice from another PC on the same Wi‑Fi: `npm run dev:lan` (writes `.env.lan` + `livekit.runtime.yaml`; no hand-edited IPs).
# Pin the address if auto-detect picks the wrong NIC: ECHO_DEV_LAN_IP=192.168.1.50
# Windows inbound rules (once, elevated): npm run lan:firewall
# Undo LAN overrides: `npm run lan:clear` then `npm run db:up` (before `npm run prod`, clear `.env.lan`).
# Comma-separated if the SPA is served from more than one origin (Vite + preview, etc.).
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8080,http://localhost:4173
# Tauri desktop WebView origins for SameSite=None session cookies (comma-separated). Defaults include http://tauri.localhost and tauri://localhost (Linux + Windows) when unset.
# ECHO_DESKTOP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://tauri.localhost,https://tauri.localhost,tauri://localhost
# Desktop build/run: releases/README.md → docs/operations/desktop-*.md

# Backend storage (single source of truth)
# - memory: dev-only mockdb (no Postgres)
# - postgres: persisted auth + Echo data (requires DATABASE_URL)
# ECHO_BACKEND_STORAGE=memory
# ECHO_BACKEND_STORAGE=postgres

# Database (required when ECHO_BACKEND_STORAGE=postgres)
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/echo

# Legacy (deprecated): USE_MOCK_DB is no longer a selector. Prefer ECHO_BACKEND_STORAGE.
# USE_MOCK_DB=true

# Docker Postgres (repo root: `npm run db:up`). Compose substitutes these from `.env`:
# POSTGRES_USER=echo
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=echo_dev
# POSTGRES_DB=echo
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://echo:echo_dev@localhost:5432/echo

# NATS JetStream (optional - for Socket.IO adapter, presence, messaging)
# NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222

# PWA: Chrome “Install app” needs HTTPS in production (localhost is exempt). Serve the built SPA with TLS;
# ensure `manifest.webmanifest` is not blocked and gets a JSON-compatible Content-Type from your CDN.

# Frontend — `VITE_*` is merged from monorepo root `.env` and `frontend/.env` (see frontend/vite.config.ts).
# You can keep a single root `.env` for backend + frontend.
#
# --- Local dev (optional — in .env at monorepo root or frontend/) ---
# Leave unset in local dev: REST uses the Vite origin (e.g. http://localhost:8080) with /api proxied
# to the backend. Socket.IO connects directly to http://localhost:3000 (see frontend/src/config.ts).
# VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:3000
# VITE_SOCKET_IO_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Tauri desktop (`npm run tauri:dev` / `npm run tauri:build`): both are required — the shell is not same-origin with the API.
# VITE_ECHO_DESKTOP=1
#
# --- Production / native release (bake HTTPS API + Socket.IO; pair both vars) ---
# VITE_API_URL=https://chat-echo.com
# VITE_SOCKET_IO_URL=https://chat-echo.com
# When UI is on Vite (:8080), Socket.IO defaults to http://<host>:3000; override port if Echo is not on 3000:
# VITE_DEV_ECHO_PORT=3000
# Mock-only UI without a backend: skip Socket.IO (fewer console errors, better back/forward cache).
# VITE_DISABLE_SOCKET=true
# Native/desktop only: show Echo’s screen-share quality modal before capture. Browsers use the OS picker only.
# VITE_SCREEN_SHARE_CONFIG_MODAL=true
# Full backend + real DB: set ECHO_BACKEND_STORAGE=postgres and DATABASE_URL (see backend config).
#
# Verbose VC trace in the browser console — grep `[Echo:VC:TRACE]`. Or:
#   localStorage.setItem('echo_vc_verbose','1')
# VITE_ECHO_VC_VERBOSE=true
#
# Session diagnostics (dev): backend artifacts under `.diagnostics/sessions/`; see docs/operations/session-diagnostics.md
# ECHO_SESSION_DIAGNOSTICS=true
#
# Optional: seed a dev user + server over HTTP (backend must be running):
# npm run seed:dev
#
# Official Echo community server — new accounts are auto-joined on signup (Postgres Echo store).
# Set ECHO_OFFICIAL_SERVER_ID to the server snowflake id, or leave unset and use vanity (default `echo`).
# ECHO_OFFICIAL_SERVER_ID=
# ECHO_OFFICIAL_SERVER_VANITY=echo

# Auth secrets. JWT_SECRET signs access tokens, MFA-pending tokens, and OAuth state HMACs.
# Required in production — the server refuses blank/short/default values.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# JWT_SECRET=
# JWT_EXPIRES_IN=15m
# BCRYPT_SALT_ROUNDS=10
# REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=30

# Redis session store. In NODE_ENV=production the server requires REDIS_URL by default
# (set ECHO_REQUIRE_REDIS_IN_PRODUCTION=false only for deliberate single-process deployments).
# `npm run prod:serve` sets these to false for the backend process only (single-host smoke):
# ECHO_REQUIRE_REDIS_IN_PRODUCTION, ECHO_REQUIRE_METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN_IN_PRODUCTION,
# ECHO_REQUIRE_MEDIA_URL_HARDENING_IN_PRODUCTION, ECHO_REQUIRE_GUEST_BINDING_SECRET_IN_PRODUCTION.
# Real deployments should set REDIS_URL, ECHO_METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN, ECHO_GUEST_BINDING_SECRET,
# and media URL hardening instead of those overrides.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# ECHO_REQUIRE_REDIS_IN_PRODUCTION=   # unset in prod = default true (Redis required)

# --- Required in NODE_ENV=production (startup fails if missing / invalid) ---
# Guest binding cookie HMAC (independent of JWT_SECRET). Unset in dev derives from JWT_SECRET.
# ECHO_GUEST_BINDING_SECRET=<32+ random chars, not JWT_SECRET>
# ECHO_REQUIRE_GUEST_BINDING_SECRET_IN_PRODUCTION=   # unset in prod = default true (secret required)
# CORS_ORIGIN=https://your.app.origin   # explicit; never * or empty
# ECHO_METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN=<32+ random chars>   # GET /api/v1/metrics requires Authorization: Bearer <token>
# ECHO_REQUIRE_METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN_IN_PRODUCTION=   # unset in prod = default true
# Optional: same in-memory auth/upload network diagnostics as founder dashboard, but Bearer-authenticated
# for operators / automation (e.g. Cursor agent curl against the public API). Off by default.
# In NODE_ENV=production, enabling requires a strong token (same length rules as metrics scrape).
# ECHO_AGENT_NETWORK_DIAG_ENABLED=true
# ECHO_AGENT_NETWORK_DIAG_TOKEN=<32+ random chars>   # GET /api/v1/agent/network-diagnostics
# ECHO_MEDIA_URL_REQUIRE_HTTPS=true   # and/or set ECHO_MEDIA_URL_ALLOWED_HOSTS (comma-separated hosts)
# ECHO_REQUIRE_MEDIA_URL_HARDENING_IN_PRODUCTION=   # unset in prod = default true
# ECHO_MEDIA_URL_ALLOWED_HOSTS=cdn.example.com,r2.example.com

# Legacy Bearer auth (Authorization: Bearer <jwt>). Enabled by default for dev.
# Disable in production unless API clients depend on it — Bearer tokens cannot be
# revoked before expiry, unlike cookie sessions.
# AUTH_LEGACY_BEARER=false

# Sockets: require JWT on every connection (always on when NODE_ENV=production).
# AUTH_REQUIRE_SOCKET_TOKEN=true

# Presence TTL sweep (offline if no heartbeat/set within N minutes). Interval ms; 0 = disable sweep.
# ECHO_PRESENCE_STALE_MINUTES=5
# ECHO_PRESENCE_SWEEP_MS=60000

# Socket message rate limits (per user per channel + burst window)
# ECHO_SOCKET_MSG_PER_MINUTE=60
# ECHO_SOCKET_BURST_MAX=20
# ECHO_SOCKET_BURST_WINDOW_MS=2000
# ECHO_SOCKET_MAX_EVENTS_PER_SEC=80
# ECHO_MESSAGE_IDEMPOTENCY_MINUTES=10

# Echo public snowflake ids (ADR 002). Each backend writer needs a unique worker id 0–31.
# SNOWFLAKE_WORKER_ID=0
# SNOWFLAKE_DATACENTER_ID=1

# After cutover is stable: drop legacy (channel_id, created_at) message indexes (see docs/operations/runbooks/snowflake-cutover.md).
# ECHO_DROP_LEGACY_MESSAGE_TIMELINE_INDEXES=false
# Optional partial index (channel_id, author_id, id) if you dropped the author+created_at index.
# ECHO_CREATE_MESSAGE_AUTHOR_ID_INDEX=false

# S3-compatible presigned uploads (Echo). Optional ECHO_S3_ENDPOINT for Cloudflare R2.
# ECHO_S3_BUCKET=
# ECHO_S3_REGION=auto
# ECHO_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# ECHO_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# ECHO_S3_ENDPOINT=
# Public read URL for browsers (no trailing slash). R2: Dashboard → bucket → Settings → Public Development URL
# (e.g. https://pub-xxxxxxxx.r2.dev). Presign PUT still uses ECHO_S3_ENDPOINT; stored/displayed URLs use this.
# ECHO_S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://pub-xxxxxxxx.r2.dev
# When true, presign `publicUrl` uses same-origin GET /api/v1/echo/uploads/s3/… (session cookie) instead of
# ECHO_S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL so GIF decode / canvas can read bytes without R2 GET CORS. The SPA also rewrites
# existing *.r2.dev/echo/… URLs to that path. Default false (direct R2 reads; then R2 CORS must allow GET for your SPA origin).
# ECHO_S3_PUBLIC_READ_THROUGH_API=true
# If S3 is unset, uploads fall back to disk under ECHO_LOCAL_UPLOAD_DIR (default: data/echo-local-uploads). Set ECHO_LOCAL_UPLOADS=false to disable and keep presign 503 until S3 is configured.
# ECHO_LOCAL_UPLOAD_DIR=
# ECHO_LOCAL_UPLOADS=
# Max raw body size for disk-backed PUT /api/v1/echo/uploads/local/put (default 256 MiB).
# Tier caps above this still get presign metadata, but local mode cannot accept huge single PUT bodies without streaming.
# ECHO_LOCAL_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=268435456
#
# Subscription tier (Postgres): column auth_users.echo_plan = free | plus | black (default free). No billing webhooks yet.
# See docs/plans/echo-plan-limits-status.md
#
# --- Presigned PUT + CORS (S3 / Cloudflare R2) ---
# The SPA uploads with XMLHttpRequest PUT to the presigned URL (cross-origin). The bucket must
# answer OPTIONS with Access-Control-Allow-Origin for your site or the browser blocks the upload
# ("No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header"). Echo cannot fix this from Node — configure the bucket.
# R2: Dashboard → R2 → your bucket → Settings → CORS policy. Example JSON (edit origins):
# [
#   {
#     "AllowedOrigins": ["https://chat-echo.com", "http://localhost:8080"],
#     "AllowedMethods": ["GET", "PUT", "HEAD"],
#     "AllowedHeaders": ["*"],
#     "ExposeHeaders": ["ETag", "x-amz-request-id"],
#     "MaxAgeSeconds": 3600
#   }
# ]
# Public read URLs (ECHO_S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL) are separate from the S3 API host; CORS applies to the
# API endpoint used for PUT. Allow Content-Type (Echo sends it on the PUT).
# For **GET** on the public R2 host: if you load GIFs with `fetch`/canvas (Echo’s limited GIF playback),
# the browser also needs Access-Control-Allow-Origin on GET for your SPA origin — or enable
# ECHO_S3_PUBLIC_READ_THROUGH_API above and skip R2 GET CORS.

# Email verification (Postgres auth). Without ECHO_SMTP_HOST, verification links are logged only (see backend logs).
# Link in email points at ECHO_API_PUBLIC_URL + /api/v1/auth/verify-email?token=...
# After success, browser redirects to ECHO_APP_PUBLIC_URL + /login?emailVerified=1
#
# Local dev with Mailpit (included in docker-compose as service `mailpit`):
#   npm run db:up
#   Open http://localhost:8025 to read outbound mail. SMTP: 127.0.0.1:1025, no TLS, no auth.
# ECHO_API_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Optional extra pepper for SHA-256 of channel incoming webhook tokens (defaults to JWT_SECRET when unset).
# ECHO_CHANNEL_WEBHOOK_TOKEN_PEPPER=
# ECHO_APP_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8080
# ECHO_SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
# ECHO_SMTP_PORT=1025
# ECHO_SMTP_SECURE=false
# ECHO_SMTP_USER=
# ECHO_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# ECHO_EMAIL_FROM=Echo <noreply@localhost>
# Optional absolute URL for the logo in HTML emails (defaults to {ECHO_APP_PUBLIC_URL}/icons/pwa-192.png).
# ECHO_EMAIL_LOGO_URL=https://your-cdn.example.com/echo-email-logo.png
# Inbox "sender avatar" in Gmail etc. is not set by HTML; use BIMI (verified mark) or your provider's brand tools.
#
# Production / real SMTP (e.g. port 587 STARTTLS: ECHO_SMTP_SECURE=false; port 465 SSL: ECHO_SMTP_SECURE=true):
# ECHO_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# ECHO_SMTP_PORT=587
# ECHO_SMTP_USER=...
# ECHO_SMTP_PASSWORD=...
# ECHO_EMAIL_FROM=Echo <auth@chat-echo.com>
# ECHO_EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TOKEN_HOURS=48
# ECHO_EMAIL_VERIFICATION_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=60
#
# --- Resend (https://resend.com) — SMTP matches Echo’s nodemailer transport in backend/src/services/email/sendMail.ts ---
# Create an API key in the Resend dashboard; set ECHO_SMTP_PASSWORD to that key only on the server (never commit it).
# Verify your sending domain in Resend; ECHO_EMAIL_FROM must use an address on that domain (or Resend’s onboarding domain).
# Option A — port 465 (implicit TLS), Resend’s common recommendation:
# ECHO_SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# ECHO_SMTP_PORT=465
# ECHO_SMTP_SECURE=true
# ECHO_SMTP_USER=resend
# ECHO_SMTP_PASSWORD=<resend_api_key>
# Option B — port 587 (STARTTLS):
# ECHO_SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# ECHO_SMTP_PORT=587
# ECHO_SMTP_SECURE=false
# ECHO_SMTP_USER=resend
# ECHO_SMTP_PASSWORD=<resend_api_key>
# ECHO_EMAIL_FROM=Echo <auth@chat-echo.com>

# SMS verification (Telnyx, Postgres). Without ECHO_TELNYX_API_KEY, OTPs are logged only (see backend logs).
# Production: set ECHO_SMS_OTP_PEPPER to a long random secret whenever ECHO_TELNYX_API_KEY is set.
# ECHO_TELNYX_API_KEY=
# ECHO_TELNYX_FROM_NUMBER=+15551234567
# ECHO_SMS_OTP_PEPPER=
# ECHO_SMS_DEFAULT_REGION=US
# ECHO_SMS_OTP_TTL_MINUTES=10
# ECHO_SMS_OTP_LENGTH=6
# ECHO_SMS_OTP_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=60
# ECHO_SMS_OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# ECHO_SMS_SEND_PER_IP_PER_HOUR=20
# ECHO_SMS_SEND_PER_USER_PER_DAY=15
# ECHO_SMS_SEND_PER_PHONE_PER_DAY=8

# TOTP / authenticator-app 2FA (Postgres auth). Secrets are encrypted at rest; required in production when DATABASE_URL is set.
# 32-byte key: 64 hex characters, or standard base64 encoding exactly 32 raw bytes.
# ECHO_2FA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Optional separate pepper for recovery-code hashes (defaults to ECHO_SMS_OTP_PEPPER when set, else a dev default).
# ECHO_2FA_RECOVERY_PEPPER=
# Issuer label in authenticator apps (otpauth URI).
# ECHO_TOTP_ISSUER=Echo
# Short-lived JWT after correct password when TOTP is enabled (e.g. 5m).
# ECHO_MFA_PENDING_JWT_EXPIRES_IN=5m
# Number of one-time backup codes issued on enroll (5–20).
# ECHO_2FA_RECOVERY_CODE_COUNT=10
# Rate limit POST /api/v1/auth/login/mfa per IP per 15 minutes.
# ECHO_MFA_LOGIN_MAX_PER_IP_PER_15MIN=60

# LiveKit VC (Layer 1). Started with `npm run db:up` alongside Postgres.
# For other laptops + voice, prefer `npm run dev:lan` — it sets LIVEKIT_PUBLIC_URL / ICE / TURN via generated `.env.lan` (see scripts/sync-lan-dev.mjs).
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY and LIVEKIT_API_SECRET must match the keys in infra/livekit/livekit.yaml (Compose defaults: devkey / devsecretdevsecretdevsecret1234).
# LIVEKIT_PUBLIC_URL is the browser-reachable WebSocket URL (NOT the Docker-internal hostname). Overridden for LAN by `lan:sync`.
# Webhooks: LiveKit must POST to a public HTTPS URL reachable from the SFU (not localhost in cloud).
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=devkey
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=devsecretdevsecretdevsecret1234
# LIVEKIT_PUBLIC_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:7880
#
# --- Production (LiveKit) ---
# See docs/operations/livekit-production.md. Example SFU config: infra/livekit/livekit.production.example.yaml
# LIVEKIT_PUBLIC_URL must be wss:// in NODE_ENV=production when LiveKit env vars are set (ws:// is rejected at startup).
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=<key id matching LiveKit server keys:>
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=<secret>
# LIVEKIT_PUBLIC_URL=wss://livekit.example.com
# Webhook URL on Echo (configure this exact URL in LiveKit server webhook.urls): https://chat-echo.com/api/v1/hooks/livekit
#
# Dev TURN (docker-compose `coturn` + rtc.turn_servers in livekit.yaml): static user echo / echo_turn_dev_secret on UDP 3478.
# Open host UDP 57000–57100 for media (see docs/infra/livekit-turn.md; `npm run lan:firewall` adds the rule).
# Optional: forward LiveKit active_speakers_changed to Socket.IO as voice_active_speakers (default off; client uses ActiveSpeakersChanged).
# LIVEKIT_EMIT_ACTIVE_SPEAKERS_WEBHOOK=false
#
# TURN username/password are NOT read from .env by LiveKit YAML — copy values into infra/livekit/livekit.yaml and docker-compose coturn --user= when rotating.
# LIVEKIT_TURN_USERNAME=echo
# LIVEKIT_TURN_CREDENTIAL=echo_turn_dev_secret
#
# Verbose VC / LiveKit trace in API logs — grep `[Echo:VC:TRACE]`. Defaults on when NODE_ENV !== production
# unless you set ECHO_VC_VERBOSE_LOG=false; in production default off unless ECHO_VC_VERBOSE_LOG=true.
# ECHO_VC_VERBOSE_LOG=true

# Discord export bot (optional). Same root `.env` is loaded via `npm run bot:export -- <guildId>`.
# See bot/README.md (privileged intents, invite bot to the server).
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
# DISCORD_GUILD_ID=
# EXPORT_BASE_DIR=bot/exports
# Echo import reads bundles from ECHO_DISCORD_EXPORTS_ROOT on the API (default: backend resolves to bot/exports). `npm run dev` sets the bot’s EXPORT_BASE_DIR to that path when unset so exports land where import expects them.
# ECHO_DISCORD_EXPORTS_ROOT=
# Discord CDN → Echo rehost for imported/synced messages (Discord URLs stored first for instant display; background job mirrors to local/S3 and pushes message:media_mirror). Requires ECHO_LOCAL_UPLOAD_DIR or S3 upload config. 0 disables.
# ECHO_DISCORD_IMPORT_MEDIA_MIRROR_MS=2000
# ECHO_DISCORD_IMPORT_MEDIA_MIRROR_BATCH_SIZE=6
# ECHO_DISCORD_IMPORT_MEDIA_MIRROR_KICK_DEBOUNCE_MS=50
# Max new Discord import metadata pipelines per user per UTC day in production (first-time metadata import per server). Default 3 when unset in production; 0 = unlimited (or no limit in non-production unless set).
# ECHO_DISCORD_IMPORT_MAX_PER_USER_PER_DAY=
# After each successful export, the bot POSTs here so the web UI can resume import. The bot also GETs …/export-pending
# (same URL prefix) to export guilds you already added before the bot was online. Use the same secret on API + bot.
# `npm run dev` waits for the API on 127.0.0.1:$PORT before starting the bot (override host with ECHO_DEV_WAIT_API_HOST).
# `npm run dev` defaults URL (127.0.0.1:$PORT) + a shared dev secret when these are unset; production must set real values.
# ECHO_DISCORD_BOT_WEBHOOK_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/hooks/discord-bot/export-ready
# ECHO_DISCORD_BOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The export bot also GETs/POSTs Discord bridge hooks using this secret: /api/v1/hooks/discord-bridge/allowlist and …/inbound (Discord → Echo live sync).
# Optional: milliseconds between export-pending polls (default 45000). Set 0 to poll only once at startup.
# ECHO_DISCORD_BOT_POLL_MS=45000

# Discord user OAuth (Settings → Discord). All three are required on the Echo API process. DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN does not enable linking,
# but when set the API uses it to detect if the export bot is already in a server so “Import from Discord” can skip opening the install tab.
# Use KEY=value lines only (no leading spaces on keys). Do not paste uncommented prose between variables — it breaks .env parsing.
# Redirect URL must match Discord Developer Portal → OAuth2 → Redirects exactly (scheme included: http ≠ https).
# Local dev (Vite proxy default): use http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/discord/callback so OAuth cookies are set on the SPA origin used by /api calls.
# Local dev with direct API origin (e.g. VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:3000): use http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/discord/callback.
# HTTPS note: if localhost is plain HTTP, using https://localhost/... in Discord + .env causes browser ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR after callback.
# Production (or TLS in front of the API): use https://<host>/api/v1/auth/discord/callback in both places.
# DISCORD_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# DISCORD_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=   # server-only; never expose to the frontend; rotate if leaked
# DISCORD_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/discord/callback
# Space-separated OAuth scopes. Default (if unset) matches Echo’s built-in list (see backend `DEFAULT_DISCORD_OAUTH_SCOPES`): identify + identify.premium, email, guilds, connections, openid, relationships.read, sdk.social_layer, sdk.social_layer_presence.
# Discord lists many more scopes in Developer Portal → OAuth2 → URL Generator (bot, rpc.*, webhook.incoming, etc.); only add what your product needs — bot/RPC scopes are not the same flow as user linking.
# Some scopes need Discord approval; the linking flow degrades if an endpoint is unavailable.
# DISCORD_OAUTH_SCOPES=connections email guilds identify identify.premium openid relationships.read sdk.social_layer sdk.social_layer_presence
# Required in production when linking is enabled (64 hex or base64 32 bytes). Separate from ECHO_2FA_ENCRYPTION_KEY.
# ECHO_DISCORD_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Shared with the export bot so it can notify Echo when a guild export finishes (see ECHO_DISCORD_BOT_WEBHOOK_* near DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN).
# ECHO_DISCORD_BOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# After OAuth, browser redirects to ECHO_APP_PUBLIC_URL?discord_linked=1 (see docs/integrations/discord-user-oauth.md).

# Google Programmable Search — composer GIF popout → Images (Custom Search JSON API, image mode).
# https://developers.google.com/custom-search/v1/overview — enable “Custom Search API” on the GCP key.
# GOOGLE_CSE_CX=
# GOOGLE_CSE_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_CSE_DEFAULT_QUERY=photography
# GOOGLE_CSE_IMAGE_PAGES=2
# Cost controls (API defaults are reasonable): in-process LRU + per-IP limits.
# GOOGLE_CSE_CACHE_TTL_MS=600000
# GOOGLE_CSE_CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES=200
# GOOGLE_CSE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=10
# Daily Google CSE calls per IP (UTC); cache hits do not count. 0 = unlimited upstream budget.
# GOOGLE_CSE_UPSTREAM_MAX_PER_DAY_PER_IP=180

# Google user OAuth (see docs/infra/integrations/google-user-oauth.md). GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET is server-only.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/google/callback
